WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:03.370
In this lesson,

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:07.040
you will explore scenarios
where vector search alone may fall short.

3
00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:12.145
You will work on some of the most common
challenges you might encounter,

4
00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:16.216
and see how to effectively address them
using vector databases.

5
00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:19.552
All right. Let's get to coding.

6
00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:22.922
Choosing the best possible
embedding model is a crucial step

7
00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:26.092
to build a reliable semantic
search system.

8
00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:29.329
However, no matter the model you choose,
there are some relationships

9
00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.998
which are not
well captured by the vectors.

10
00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:35.702
Sometimes these problematic relationships
may be counterintuitive.

11
00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:38.338
So let's review them in this lesson.

12
00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:42.509
We already know that tokenization is an
important step of creating the embeddings,

13
00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:46.646
especially since it can chop our words
into multiple pieces.

14
00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:50.450
And this transform
the actual input of the transformer.

15
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.486
Subword
level tokens may have clear meaning

16
00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:56.856
that will be captured already
in the input token embeddings layer.

17
00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:01.895
Shorter or rare tokens may, on the other
hand, occur in various contexts,

18
00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:04.931
what effectively limits
the ability of the model

19
00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:07.934
to learn proper input representations.

20
00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.703
This is especially true for the prefixes

21
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:14.407
and suffixes
which are parts of different words.

22
00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:19.345
None of these tokens has a specific
meaning, and the corresponding input

23
00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:22.982
token embeddings will be rather covering
different contexts.

24
00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:26.419
It's certainly surprising, though,
that the ability of the model

25
00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:30.323
to capture the semantics of both words
will be rather limited.

26
00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:34.828
Also, some papers, such as: "Investigating
the effectiveness of BPE,

27
00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:39.933
the Power of Short Sequences",
suggest that the fewer tokens an algorithm

28
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:43.703
needs to cover the test set, the better
the results might be.

29
00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:48.074
There are various other problems you may
face while implementing semantic search.

30
00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:52.779
Let's see what are the most common
misconceptions and how to avoid them.

31
00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:57.183
There are some myths about the magical
capabilities of the semantic search.

32
00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:01.454
Unfortunately, it fell short
if the tokenizer cannot handle input data

33
00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:02.188
properly.

34
00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:06.459
Typically, you don't consider
the embeddings assigned to each individual

35
00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:10.230
token, but you just take a single vector
per whole text.

36
00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:15.068
In this lesson, you will use
the same sentence transformer like before.

37
00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:17.170
You will also need the

38
00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.607
tokenizer
to see how texts are split into tokens.

39
00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:24.244
Let's access it.

40
00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:27.447
You might be surprised to see that

41
00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:31.751
some characters which are commonly used
nowadays are not

42
00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:34.721
That well capture by some of the embedding
models.

43
00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:38.825
The happy face emoji is not translated
to any meaningful token,

44
00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:41.861
and we should not expect it to capture

45
00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:44.864
the real emotions
of the person who wrote this text

46
00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:48.501
if we use this particular model.
Things may change

47
00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:51.905
if we just describe them using text.

48
00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:54.941
There seems to be a token
corresponding to being happy,

49
00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:59.512
but only if we use regular letters,
not emojis.

50
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.183
How about different emotions?

51
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:07.987
Sad Face
and happy face are both not recognized

52
00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:11.391
and converted into the same unknown token.

53
00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:15.695
If our documents only differ
in that single word,

54
00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:19.799
then the transformer input
will be identical in both cases.

55
00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:23.102
However, when we use regular letters,

56
00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:27.440
there is a corresponding token found.

57
00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:30.810
Again, our model struggles with emojis

58
00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.813
and probably with
some other sets of characters.

59
00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:37.283
If you want to use it on social media
data, you'd better check

60
00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:40.353
how many unknown tokens there are.

61
00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:43.856
Many businesses
use specific domain terminology.

62
00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:47.393
For example, Broadcom BCM2712

63
00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:52.098
is a processor used in Raspberry Pi.

64
00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.201
If someone asked for this device,

65
00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:58.705
you would love to see this processor
in the set of results.

66
00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:02.575
Unfortunately, the name of

67
00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:06.312
it is just cut into multiple subword
level tokens.

68
00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.017
You might also consider semantic search
to handle synonyms as well.

69
00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:14.487
But if you check how titles are tokenized,

70
00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:19.492
you will see there are lots of subword
level tokens and probably not

71
00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:22.929
that much meaning of their corresponding
input token embeddings.

72
00:04:23.563 --> 00:04:26.733
If your users will just accidentally omit

73
00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:31.704
the double "C" in the word accommodation,
then the whole text

74
00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:35.541
will be just transformed
into a completely different set of tokens.

75
00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:39.112
Many people
also believe that vector embeddings

76
00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:42.382
should capture the numbers and produce
meaningful results.

77
00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:45.451
For example, when two items

78
00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:48.021
have similar prices.

79
00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:51.090
There are various ways
of how to define price.

80
00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:54.927
However, the overlap of the tokens
is the highest between

81
00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:57.930
the first and the fourth example.

82
00:04:58.765 --> 00:05:01.768
Even though the prices
are completely different.

83
00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:05.972
When it comes to dates, there are also
various ways to describe the same day.

84
00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:09.609
If you have multiple different formats
in your system,

85
00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:12.779
then the tokenization

86
00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:15.114
will also be completely different.

87
00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:17.684
That still doesn't mean
the representations

88
00:05:17.684 --> 00:05:18.918
will be far from each other.

89
00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:21.154
That's something we need to verify.

90
00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:25.725
The first two examples
represent the same date, but they are

91
00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:30.725
obviously the least similar in terms
of how they're converted into tokens.

92
00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:34.300
Practically, we never calculate

93
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:39.105
embeddings of single words,
but rather sentences or paragraphs.

94
00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:42.175
The impact of a single token
is not that high,

95
00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:44.777
but it might be significant in some cases.

96
00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:48.848
Let's check the same examples like before,
but this time the similarity

97
00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:52.352
of the output text embeddings.

98
00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:56.222
We will display the similarity
as a heatmap.

99
00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:02.495
Emojis do not have any token
embeddings assigned,

100
00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:07.567
and that results in a different emojis
being encoded as unknown token.

101
00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:11.671
Eventually, it won't make a difference
if someone is happy

102
00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:14.974
or sad,
as long as they use emojis to describe it.

103
00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.811
Let's also check how typos

104
00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:21.914
may impact the overall similarity.

105
00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:24.917
Similarity of both sentences
is not that high.

106
00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:29.389
Even though any human would see
these sentences mean the same thing.

107
00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:33.292
As a next example,
you will check the numerical values.

108
00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:38.498
The biggest overlap of the tokens

109
00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:41.501
also results in the highest similarity.

110
00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:47.301
Increasing the price from $55 to 559

111
00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:51.077
does not change the meaning too much.

112
00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:54.013
Another example you checked for the tokenization

113
00:06:54.013 --> 00:06:57.016
was about the dates.

114
00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:03.523
The same format of the date

115
00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:06.993
seems to have highest impact
on the similarity.

116
00:07:07.827 --> 00:07:12.165
Even though the first two examples
describe the same date,

117
00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:15.902
their similarity score is the lowest.

118
00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:20.440
The problems you just realized

119
00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:23.943
might not hold true for all the embedding
models available out there.

120
00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:27.079
That's why
this kind of evaluation is important.

121
00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:31.784
Otherwise, you don't know what specific
types of queries are just not supported.

122
00:07:32.485 --> 00:07:35.822
Let's take another model
and see how well it solves them.

123
00:07:35.855 --> 00:07:39.091
OpenAI embeddings are commonly used,
so we'll check them now.

124
00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:44.130
The Tiktoken package is an OpenAI fast
implementation of the byte-pair encoding.

125
00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:48.234
Let's check the vocabulary size
used in the model you will use.

126
00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:52.772
The vocabulary is three times bigger than
the sentence transformer we used so far.

127
00:07:52.872 --> 00:07:57.872
Let's see how well it works
for the same cases and start with emojis.

128
00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:02.982
It seems that emoji is transformed
to some tokens, but on the byte level.

129
00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:06.252
Obviously it will be different
if we just use words.

130
00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:08.754
The first difference you can see

131
00:08:08.754 --> 00:08:12.992
is that the OpenAI tokenizer was trained
on some additional sets of Unicode.

132
00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:16.162
Thus it should handle them better.

133
00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:20.032
Let's
see if the Raspberry Pi and its processor

134
00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:23.035
was also commonly mentioned
in the training data.

135
00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:26.539
It seems the CPU wasn't mentioned too
often.

136
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.610
Still, that doesn't mean
the representation will be bad.

137
00:08:31.444 --> 00:08:34.447
Let's now check how typos

138
00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.450
impact the tokenization.

139
00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:40.553
Different prices will also be encoded
differently.

140
00:08:41.254 --> 00:08:44.257
Let's check the tokenization out.

141
00:08:45.892 --> 00:08:48.895
Last but not least,
different date formats.

142
00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:55.801
After checking the tokenization
of the problematic cases,

143
00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:58.804
let's check if these problems
also impact the similarity.

144
00:08:59.038 --> 00:09:02.808
First of all,
we have to connect to the OpenAI services.

145
00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:06.846
Now, if we pass the same

146
00:09:06.846 --> 00:09:10.016
texts using both emojis and regular text,

147
00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:14.153
we can calculate the cosine distance
between all of these examples

148
00:09:15.254 --> 00:09:16.222
and check

149
00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:19.292
how the similarity of all the examples
look like.

150
00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:24.725
Emojis are supported by the model
and the same emotion described with text

151
00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:28.501
and graphical representation
seem to keep the highest

152
00:09:28.501 --> 00:09:31.470
similarity of the sentences.

153
00:09:31.571 --> 00:09:34.574
Let's check the typos and their impact.

154
00:09:36.509 --> 00:09:37.410
Similarities

155
00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.413
are definitely higher
than for the sentence transformer.

156
00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.283
If you think using the OpenAI
embeddings solves all the problems,

157
00:09:44.517 --> 00:09:47.453
you'd better do some more evaluations.

158
00:09:47.453 --> 00:09:50.456
Another example was about prices

159
00:09:50.456 --> 00:09:53.459
and numbers in general.

160
00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:55.695
Definitely,

161
00:09:55.695 --> 00:10:00.695
OpenAI embeddings seem to handle
the numbers better than the sentence

162
00:10:00.967 --> 00:10:02.702
transformer

163
00:10:02.702 --> 00:10:06.739
does not necessarily generalize
to all the numbers, but

164
00:10:06.806 --> 00:10:09.075
this is already promising.

165
00:10:09.075 --> 00:10:12.445
When we perform the same process
for the dates,

166
00:10:14.013 --> 00:10:15.615
different formats

167
00:10:15.615 --> 00:10:18.985
are still problematic
and must be solved differently.

168
00:10:19.819 --> 00:10:24.390
It's better to consider some normalization
if that's the case for your data.

169
00:10:24.991 --> 00:10:27.960
Semantic search is not all you need.

170
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.430
You should also pay more attention
to the additional constraints

171
00:10:31.764 --> 00:10:33.366
typical for the domain.

172
00:10:33.366 --> 00:10:38.337
You will now connect to Qdrant
and check an example of a real problem

173
00:10:38.337 --> 00:10:41.340
you might encounter
while working with semantic search.

174
00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:45.678
A Qdrant server should be already running
in the environment, and connecting to

175
00:10:45.678 --> 00:10:48.781
it is as simple
as creating a client instance.

176
00:10:49.048 --> 00:10:51.150
Let's
also check the list of the collections

177
00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:53.486
we have to make sure
the environment is fresh.

178
00:10:56.155 --> 00:10:59.158
Your data
will consist of textual descriptions

179
00:10:59.492 --> 00:11:02.328
of different pieces of clothes
and their prices.

180
00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:06.065
Don't take it too seriously as it was
generated by one of the GPT's.

181
00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.935
You can now create a collection

182
00:11:09.935 --> 00:11:14.073
that will keep the data encoded
with the sentence transformer we use.

183
00:11:14.607 --> 00:11:19.512
It produces vectors with 384 dimensions
and that's the size you'll set.

184
00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:23.149
Cosine distance is a metric
you want to use to compare the vector

185
00:11:23.149 --> 00:11:24.750
similarity.

186
00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.019
The upsert methods of the client

187
00:11:27.019 --> 00:11:29.822
helps to put the data in a collection.

188
00:11:29.822 --> 00:11:32.858
Each point has an ID, a vector,

189
00:11:33.459 --> 00:11:36.896
and some metadata that we call payload,

190
00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:41.929
in Qdrant's terminology.
Our points are ready to be searched over.

191
00:11:43.703 --> 00:11:46.706
The query has to be encoded

192
00:11:46.739 --> 00:11:49.742
with the same model
as we used for the documents.

193
00:11:49.775 --> 00:11:54.775
And now imagine you want to find a piece
of cloth suitable for winter.

194
00:11:55.448 --> 00:11:57.717
This is a use case
in which semantic search

195
00:11:57.717 --> 00:12:01.020
should shine over the lexical search,
as none of the query

196
00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:04.023
words has been ever used in the product
descriptions.

197
00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:07.993
Our results seem to be okay,
but in some cases

198
00:12:08.461 --> 00:12:11.197
we may have some additional requirements.

199
00:12:11.197 --> 00:12:15.367
Imagine one of your users
is looking for something below $40.

200
00:12:16.202 --> 00:12:20.139
They would probably put this kind of query
for cold weather under $40.

201
00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:24.744
Unfortunately, the price requirement
was not properly handled.

202
00:12:25.244 --> 00:12:28.247
The semantic search does not guarantee
that it will be captured.

203
00:12:28.981 --> 00:12:31.984
The payload is a solution
to this kind of problems.

204
00:12:32.451 --> 00:12:35.054
Vector databases generally provide

205
00:12:35.054 --> 00:12:39.158
additional mechanism to apply filters
next to the semantic similarity.

206
00:12:39.191 --> 00:12:42.862
If your vector database
has a concept of metadata indexes,

207
00:12:43.229 --> 00:12:47.967
it's always good to use them to keep
this additional filtering efficient.

208
00:12:48.534 --> 00:12:52.938
A good practice in Qdrant is creating
a payload index on a filter fields we use.

209
00:12:53.272 --> 00:12:56.809
In our case, an index on the price
field makes a lot of sense.

210
00:12:57.843 --> 00:12:59.011
From now on,

211
00:12:59.011 --> 00:13:02.014
when we have strict
price level requirements

212
00:13:02.748 --> 00:13:05.751
which constrain
the set of results using filters.

213
00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:09.321
That's the only way to ensure

214
00:13:09.321 --> 00:13:12.892
we always provide
a relevant set of results

215
00:13:13.392 --> 00:13:17.863
given a user query.
All the results returned by our system,

216
00:13:17.863 --> 00:13:20.299
fulfill our criteria.

217
00:13:20.299 --> 00:13:23.669
You can also play with your own queries,
but generally

218
00:13:23.903 --> 00:13:27.439
semantic search should work
even for some more sophisticated

219
00:13:27.439 --> 00:13:31.277
ones without any overlap
with the documents we have.

220
00:13:31.744 --> 00:13:35.581
Still, there is a challenge in
how to extract these additional filtres

221
00:13:35.581 --> 00:13:39.552
from the query that will require
changing the UI of the system

222
00:13:39.919 --> 00:13:42.922
or applying some NLP techniques
to detect them.

223
00:13:43.055 --> 00:13:46.392
Eventually, the LLM's will also
be really good at extracting

224
00:13:46.392 --> 00:13:49.395
these constraints
into a structured output.

225
00:13:49.562 --> 00:13:53.032
Overall, that's another challenge
we want to discuss in this course.

226
00:13:53.666 --> 00:13:54.633
In the next lesson,

227
00:13:54.633 --> 00:13:59.305
you will learn how to ensure
your semantic search system works well.

228
00:14:00.272 --> 00:14:01.841
Let's discuss quality.

229
00:14:01.841 --> 00:14:02.975
See you in the next lesson.

